{"editorial_reason":"The standard definition of equivalence relation first requires a binary relation on a set, interpreted as a subset of its Cartesian square. The existing subset-based binary-relation meaning provides the correct underlying object.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.set63.relation-reflexive-symmetric-transitive","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.01","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Relation","source_phrase":"subset of S×S","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ARelation","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.binary-relation-subset"}
{"editorial_reason":"A subset R of S×S refers to the Cartesian product of a set with itself. The independently screened binary Cartesian product meaning gives the precise collection of ordered pairs on which reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity are asserted.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.set63.relation-reflexive-symmetric-transitive","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.02","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Relation","source_phrase":"S×S","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary"}
{"editorial_reason":"An equivalence class is defined only after fixing an equivalence relation on S. This relation determines which y belong to the representative's class and thereby makes the set-of-related-elements definition unambiguous.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.set63.set-of-related-elements","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.03","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Class","source_phrase":"equivalence relation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Relation","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.relation-reflexive-symmetric-transitive"}
{"editorial_reason":"A quotient set is the collection of all equivalence classes of the given underlying set. The classes themselves must have been defined before the quotient of the set can be formed.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.set63.set-of-equivalence-classes","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.04","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AQuotient_Set","source_phrase":"equivalence classes","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Class","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.set-of-related-elements"}
{"editorial_reason":"The quotient is taken with respect to a specified equivalence relation. Different relations may yield distinct quotient partitions of the same underlying set; the relation is an essential component of the construction.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.set63.set-of-equivalence-classes","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.05","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AQuotient_Set","source_phrase":"equivalence relation","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Relation","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.relation-reflexive-symmetric-transitive"}
{"editorial_reason":"When N includes zero, the strictly positive indexing convention can be identified with N without the distinguished zero. This link preserves the explicit choice of a starting-index convention without equating the two underlying sets.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.set63.natural-positive-convention","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.06","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ANatural_Number","source_phrase":"N_{>0}","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ANatural_Number","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.natural-including-zero"}
{"editorial_reason":"The concrete integer construction represents a signed difference by a pair (a,b) of natural numbers. The reviewed zero-inclusive convention supplies both the natural zero and the entries needed for the resulting difference-class quotient.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.integer59.integer-equivalence-class","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.07","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AInteger/Formal_Definition","source_phrase":"natural numbers","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ANatural_Number","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.natural-including-zero"}
{"editorial_reason":"In the pair construction an integer is represented by an equivalence class of N×N under equal-difference equivalence. The independently sourced class definition specifies what such an equivalence class is without invoking integer arithmetic.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.integer59.integer-equivalence-class","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.08","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AInteger/Formal_Definition","source_phrase":"equivalence class","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AEquivalence_Class","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.set-of-related-elements"}
{"editorial_reason":"The set of integers in this concrete construction is the quotient of N×N by the specified equivalence relation. The quotient-set definition explains why its elements are classes, not individual natural-number pairs.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.integer59.integer-equivalence-class","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.09","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AInteger/Formal_Definition","source_phrase":"quotient set","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AQuotient_Set","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.set63.set-of-equivalence-classes"}
{"editorial_reason":"The integer difference construction begins with pairs in the Cartesian square of the natural numbers. The existing Cartesian-product meaning identifies the exact source set prior to taking equivalence classes.","from_formulation":"form.wave2.integer59.integer-equivalence-class","human_verified":false,"id":"rel.wave2.set64.10","relation_type":"definition_uses","review_state":"screened","reviewed_by":"assistant_independent_set_foundation_relation_screen_2026_10_20","source_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3AInteger/Formal_Definition","source_phrase":"N×N","target_locator":"https://proofwiki.org/wiki/Definition%3ACartesian_Product","target_meaning":"meaning.wave2.foundation.cartesian-product-binary"}
